 The Michelson interferometer produces fringes due to interference of light waves reflected from the two mirrors. When one mirror is moved by a distance \( \Delta d \), the path difference changes by \( 2\Delta d \) (since the light travels to the mirror and back). The periodic variation in fringe contrast occurs when the path difference changes by one wavelength, but for a doublet, it occurs when the path difference changes by the difference in wavelengths \( \Delta \lambda \).

The condition for the periodic variation in fringe contrast is given by:
\[ 2\Delta d = \frac{\lambda^2}{\Delta \lambda} \]

Here, \( \Delta d = 0.44 \, \text{mm} = 0.00044 \, \text{m} \) and the average wavelength \( \lambda = 676 \, \text{nm} = 676 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \).

First, convert \( \Delta d \) to meters:
\[ 0.44 \, \text{mm} = 0.00044 \, \text{m} \]

Now, solve for \( \Delta \lambda \):
\[ 2 \times 0.00044 = \frac{(676 \times 10^{-9})^2}{\Delta \lambda} \]
\[ 0.00088 = \frac{456976 \times 10^{-18}}{\Delta \lambda} \]
\[ \Delta \lambda = \frac{456976 \times 10^{-18}}{0.00088} \]
\[ \Delta \lambda \approx 5.21 \times 10^{-9} \, \text{m} \]
\[ \Delta \lambda \approx 5.21 \, \text{nm} \]

The two wavelengths are then:
